Q1. Demonstrate the use of a constructor with the this keyword to resolve ambiguity between instance variables and local method parameters.

**Instance Variable**-- A variable declared inside a class but outside any method or constructor.

class Student {
    String name;   // instance variable
}

**Local Parameter**---- A parameter is a special type of local variable that exists only inside a method or constructor.

Student(String name)
Here:
name is the constructor parameter.

CODE--->
class Student{
    String name;
    
    Student(String name){
        this.name = name;     //instance variable = parameter, this.name refers to the instance variable, while name refers to the                                        constructor parameter.
    }
    void display(){
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
class Codechef
{
	public static void main (String[] args) throws java.lang.Exception
	{
		Student s1 = new Student("Alex");
		s1.display();

	}
}

Q2. Discuss the purpose of the break and continue statements within control flow loops in Java. When is using a break considered mandatory?
break -- Terminates the loop immediately and transfers control to the statement after the loop.
continue -- Skips the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.

When is break mandatory?

In a traditional switch statement, break is generally required to prevent 'fall-through' into the next case.

switch(day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
}
Without break, execution continues into subsequent cases.

