Q4. Analyze the difference between == operator and the .equals() method when comparing objects in Java

| Feature  | `==`                                            | `.equals()`                             |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Compares | References (memory addresses)                   | Object contents                         |
| Checks   | Whether two references point to the same object | Whether two objects are logically equal |
| Used for | Primitive values and reference comparison       | Object content comparison               |

EXAMPLE-
String s1 = new String("Java");
String s2 = new String("Java");
System.out.println(s1 == s2);
System.out.println(s1.equals(s2));

Output
false
true

Explanation-
s1 == s2 is false because they are different objects.
s1.equals(s2) is true because both contain the same text



